August 20, 1987

Microsoft Operating System / 2
Device Driver Development Kit

These two diskettes contain the initial release of
the Microsoft Operating System / 2 Device Driver
Kit.  This kit is intended to be used in conjunction
with the Software Development Kit for OS/2 version 1.0.

The directory structure on the disks should be duplicated
on your development system, as there are dependencies in
makefiles, and linker response files that rely on this 
structure.

It is essential to have the SDK installed on the system
you intend to use the DDDK on as it relies on files supplied 
with the SDK.  In some cases, depending on your directory
structure, it may be necessary to modify the makefiles
and linker files in the device driver directories in order to
successfully build the sample device drivers.

NOTES and ERRATA:

  1 In DDDK Guide section 3.2 (page 15), "Setting Up the DOS-
    resident Debugger", some additional notes are necessary.
    First, the bootable disk referred to in step 1 is a copy of
    the boot disk provided with the SDK.  The three steps must
    be repeated for the os2bio.com file.

  2 The MAPSYM utility supplied with this kit will not run in
    protect mode.

  3 In DDDK Guide section 3.7 (pp. 56-58) reference is made 
    to "Debugging with DBPRT Statements."  Please disregard
    this section as DBPRT is not supported in this release.

  4 On page 66, Section 4.2.1, "Running the ADD Tests", the
    message regarding switching displays is not supported 
    in this release.  All debug output will go to the debug
    terminal.

  5 The device driver supplied with this kit, WILL NOT
    produce a binary that matches the SDK disk driver,
    it is, however, the same driver but of a later revision.

  6 On page 105, step 8, type G SETUP_DEVICE (not SET_UP_DEVICE)
    and type P 2 before examining/modifying the baud rate in AX.

  7 On page 105, step 10, G should be repeated to reach the desired
    breakpoint.

  8 On page 143, step 14, if carry is set after the call, type
    BP CS:IP and G until carry is not set before continuing.
